window.BANK = window.BANK || {};
window.BANK["ccdv-p"] = (window.BANK["ccdv-p"] || []).concat([
{ id:"d2-01", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"exam-guide-sample",
  question:"A customer-facing Claude service runs with a fallback policy that retries any failed request against a second model with exponential backoff. After a model update, the team sees an unexpected cost spike and a cluster of fallback responses that are shorter than expected. Traces show three distinct stop conditions mixed in the same retry path: rate-limit errors, responses that stopped at the maximum output length, and responses in which the model declined the request. Which change best improves reliability?",
  options:["Increase the retry count and widen the backoff window so that more requests eventually succeed regardless of why they failed.",
    "Branch on the stop condition: back off and retry rate limits, raise the output budget on length stops, and route declined requests to a safety-handling path.",
    "Route all traffic to the fallback model permanently, since it is answering the retried requests and the primary model changed with the update.",
    "Disable the fallback entirely and return the first error to the caller so that the cost spike stops and failures become visible."],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Official guide rationale: the three stop conditions are different failure classes and require different handling. Retrying a rate-limited request with backoff is correct; retrying a length-stopped response reproduces the truncation and wastes tokens; retrying a declined request repeats the refusal at additional cost and may be inappropriate. A single undifferentiated retry path is the root cause. Increasing retries (A) amplifies the cost spike; permanent rerouting (C) and disabling the fallback (D) trade one failure for another without addressing the classification problem." },
{ id:"d2-02", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A team retires its own search integration, a client tool its agent loop executed and answered with tool_result blocks, and switches to Anthropic's web_search tool. The loop still dispatches on tool names. Long verification requests now occasionally come back unfinished, and the team does not want to pay twice for searches already run. What should the loop do?",
  options:["Keep the old client search tool next to web_search, and route the long verifications to the old tool",
    "Remove the web_search branch, and on pause_turn resend the original user message with the same tools",
    "Remove the web_search branch, and on pause_turn send the paused content back with the same tools",
    "Remove the web_search branch, and raise max_tokens so that long verifications fit in one response"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Web search is a server tool: Anthropic runs it, so the loop neither handles the call nor returns a tool_result. An unfinished long turn is pause_turn: send the paused content back as the assistant message with the same tools and the turn resumes where it stopped. Keeping the old tool (A) works but keeps the integration the team is retiring. Resending the original message (B) restarts the turn and pays for the searches again. max_tokens (D) caps output length; it does not prevent a server-side pause." },
{ id:"d2-03", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A health insurer runs under a zero-data-retention agreement and must stay HIPAA-eligible. Its claims assistant uses web_search_20260209 with an allowed_domains list of regulator sites, on a Claude 4.6-or-later model with default tool settings. Compliance blocks the release. The fix must keep answers limited to the regulator sites and must not require new infrastructure. What should change?",
  options:["Switch to web_search_20250305, and drop allowed_domains to keep the tool entry minimal",
    "Keep web_search_20260209 and its allowed_domains list, and set allowed_callers to direct",
    "Keep the default settings, and set response_inclusion to excluded on the web search tool",
    "Replace web search with a client tool that calls a new search proxy limited to the regulators"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"web_search_20260209 enables dynamic filtering by default; it runs in code execution and is not ZDR- or HIPAA-eligible. Restricting the tool to direct calls disables it while the allowed_domains list keeps working. The basic version (A) would also be eligible, but dropping allowed_domains loses the restriction to regulator sites. response_inclusion (C) trims what the response returns and exists from web_search_20260318; it does not change where filtering runs. A custom search proxy (D) is new infrastructure." },
{ id:"d2-04", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A standards-checking service calls the Claude API with web_search_20260209 and relies on dynamic filtering to keep context small. To survive Claude API outages it adds a fallback path to Claude models on Google Cloud, Google-managed deployment, where its requests are rejected at validation. Each request verifies several references, and answers must stay grounded in live sources on both paths. What is the right change?",
  options:["Send web_search_20250305 on the fallback path with max_uses set to 1 to keep its context small",
    "Route the fallback to the Claude API in another region and keep web_search_20260209 as it is",
    "Drop web search on the fallback path and answer from model knowledge with a recency disclaimer",
    "Send web_search_20250305 on the fallback path and budget for the larger results in its context"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Google-managed deployments support only the basic web search version, so the fallback sends web_search_20250305 and accepts that results enter the context unfiltered: per-platform feature parity. Capping searches at one (A) keeps the context small but cannot verify several references per request. Another Claude API region (B) does not help when the outage is the Claude API itself. Dropping search (C) breaks the grounding requirement." },
{ id:"d2-05", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A reference-checking job loops over the content of each web_search_tool_result block to log result URLs. During traffic peaks it crashes with a TypeError, although the HTTP status of those responses is 200. Every failed search must be recorded so that its reference is retried in the next run. What should the code do?",
  options:["Check whether the block's content is a list; if not, log its error_code and queue the reference",
    "Wrap the loop in a try/except that skips the failing block and moves on to the next result block",
    "Check the HTTP status before parsing, and retry the whole request whenever it is not 200",
    "Check stop_reason for pause_turn; send the paused content back until the results arrive"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Web search errors arrive with HTTP 200: the block's content is then a single web_search_tool_result_error object with an error_code (too_many_requests under load) instead of a list. Checking the type handles it and keeps the failure for retry. Skipping the block (B) stops the crash but loses the failure record. An HTTP status check (C) never fires, since the status is 200. pause_turn (D) is a different condition and does not explain an error object in the block." },
{ id:"d2-06", domain:2, type:"multi", select:2, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An engineering consultancy carries professional liability for every standard it cites. Before a proposal ships, Claude must verify each clause reference against the standards bodies' and governments' own sites, with no other site able to influence the result, and reviewers must see which pages each verification relied on. Which TWO choices meet these requirements?",
  options:["Use blocked_domains for the known forums and reseller sites, and allow all other sites",
    "List the bare domains of the standards bodies and government sites in allowed_domains",
    "Name the standards bodies and government sites in the system prompt as the only sources to trust",
    "Collect the title and url of each web search result and attach them to the checked references",
    "Log each search query Claude issued, and attach the query text to the checked references"],
  correct:[1,3],
  explanation:"allowed_domains limits results to the named sites (bare domains, subdomains included), and the web_search_tool_result blocks list each result's title and url, which shows reviewers the pages used. A blocklist (A) still lets every unlisted site through. A system prompt (C) asks the model to prefer sources but does not restrict results. Query text (E) shows what was asked, not which pages answered." },
{ id:"d2-07", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A nightly job verifies 200 references in a proposal library. Some responses end with stop_reason pause_turn, and the job stores them as finished, so several verdicts are missing. The team wants each request to complete without losing searches already done. What should the job do on pause_turn?",
  options:["Raise max_tokens, resend the original user message alone, and accept the result of that second attempt",
    "Append a user message asking Claude to continue, resend with the same tools, and repeat up to a cap",
    "Append the response content as an assistant message, resend with the same tools, and repeat up to a cap",
    "Treat pause_turn as a transient error, resend the original request, and back off exponentially between tries"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"pause_turn means the server paused a long turn: the continuation is the paused content sent back as the assistant message, with the same tools, checking stop_reason each time and capping the loop. Resending the original request (A, D) discards the searches already run and pays for them again, and a new user message (B) breaks the paused turn instead of resuming it." },
{ id:"d2-08", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"To cap spend, a team sets max_uses to 5 on web search. In one request with no pause, the response holds a max_uses_exceeded error, yet the usage block reports only 3 billed searches. What explains the difference?",
  options:["Searches that return several result pages count once per page toward max_uses only",
    "Searches run by dynamic filtering count toward max_uses but are billed as code execution",
    "Searches from earlier requests of the conversation count toward the current max_uses",
    "Searches that fail count toward max_uses but are left out of the billed search count"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Failed searches count toward max_uses and are not billed, which reconciles 5 uses with 3 billed searches. Each search is one use whatever the number of results (A), dynamic filtering adds no code execution charge (B), and the limit applies per turn: only a turn spanning several requests, as after pause_turn, carries earlier searches, and the stem rules that out (C)." },
{ id:"d2-09", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A developer reads resp.content[0].text after a web-search-enabled request and stores it as the verification verdict. Many stored verdicts turn out to be a single sentence announcing that Claude will check the references. The stored verdict must contain every finding Claude wrote; keeping the announcement sentence is acceptable. What should the code store?",
  options:["The text of the last text block, which comes after the final search",
    "The text of every text block in the response, joined in order",
    "The text of the text blocks that carry citations, joined in order",
    "The text of the first and the last text blocks, joined in that order"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"With web search, Claude's answer arrives in several text blocks interleaved with the searches, so the complete answer is every text block joined in order. The last block (A), the cited blocks only (C) or the first and last blocks (D) each drop findings written in other blocks, including conclusions that carry no citation." },
{ id:"d2-10", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An administrator restricted web search in the Claude Console to an organization list of domains and will not extend it for a single team. A team's requests must reach a newly accredited standards body, whose standards are also published under one path of a government site that is on the organization list. The team's requests must stay limited to its own short list, and answers must come from the live site. What should the team do?",
  options:["Add the government site's path for that body's standards to the request's allowed_domains",
    "Ask the Console administrator to add the body's own domain to the organization's domain list",
    "Remove allowed_domains from the request so that the organization's whole list applies to it",
    "Paste the body's published standards into the system prompt of each request that needs them"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"A request-level allowed_domains list must stay within the organization's list, and web search accepts a path in an entry, so the government site's path for the body's standards is both allowed and narrow. Asking the administrator (B) is the usual route, ruled out here. Removing the request list (C) widens the team's search to the whole organization list. Pasting the standards (D) answers from a copy rather than the live site." },
{ id:"d2-11", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A deployment service exposes each environment as its own MCP endpoint with its own token, and Claude Code runs a staging pipeline job. The job must release to staging on its own. Releasing to production must stay impossible from that job even if a hook script fails, and the job must not hold credentials it does not use. Which setup meets these goals?",
  options:["Configure one MCP server per environment, give the job all the tokens, and allowlist only staging's tools",
    "Configure a single MCP server, and add a PreToolUse hook that denies releases aimed at production",
    "Configure a single MCP server, and keep production jobs on their own runner with release disallowed",
    "Configure one MCP server per environment, give the job only the staging token, and allowlist its tools"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Permission rules name an MCP server and tool, not the tool's arguments, so separate servers let an allowlist tell staging from production; holding only the staging token adds a credential barrier that cannot fail open. Holding all the tokens (A) satisfies the allowlist but gives the job credentials it does not use. A hook (B) can read arguments, but if it fails to run the call is not denied. Restricting production jobs (C) leaves the staging job able to reach production through the shared server." },
{ id:"d2-12", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"In the production deployment setup, release is gated by the release manager's hook. A reviewer proposes gating rollback behind the same hook for symmetry. The closing-the-loop control must restore service within minutes of a breached control band, at any hour, and the rollback path must be proven before it is needed. Which policy fits?",
  options:["Gate rollback behind the release manager's hook, and exercise it in staging",
    "Keep rollback as a runbook that the on-call engineer runs by hand when paged",
    "Keep rollback pre-approved in production, and exercise it regularly in staging",
    "Pre-approve rollback in business hours, and gate it behind the hook at night"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Rollback is a runbook approved in advance: one command the agent can run, rehearsed regularly in staging, so the closing-the-loop control can call it without waiting for a person. Gating it (A), handing it to on-call (B) or gating it at night (D) puts a human in the recovery path at exactly the hours the target covers." },
{ id:"d2-13", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A hand-written agent loop pushes one user message per tool_result. When Claude calls get_weather and get_forecast in the same turn, the next request fails. The team must keep its own loop, which also writes its audit log, and wants both lookups in a single turn for latency. What should the loop send?",
  options:["One tool_result per message, with parallel tool use disabled for each request of the loop",
    "One user message per tool_result, sent in the order in which the tool_use blocks appeared",
    "One user message holding a tool_result block for each tool_use block, each tied to its id",
    "The results through the SDK's tool runner, which sends them back for the loop on its own"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Every tool_use block of the assistant turn needs its tool_result, tied by tool_use_id, and they go back together in the next user message. Disabling parallel tool use (A) works but adds a turn per call, against the latency goal. Separate user messages (B) leave the first follow-up missing a result, which is the failure observed. The tool runner (D) handles the loop correctly but replaces the hand-written loop the team must keep." },
{ id:"d2-14", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An application uses the Messages API's MCP connector to give Claude a Slack MCP server that exposes dozens of tools, including posting and deleting messages. Claude must be able to search messages and list channels only, the unused tool definitions should stay out of context, and the team does not want to maintain its own Slack integration. How should the mcp_toolset be configured?",
  options:["Set default_config enabled to false, and enable search_messages and list_channels in configs",
    "Keep all tools enabled, and tell Claude in the system prompt not to post or delete any messages",
    "Remove the mcp_toolset entry, and keep only the server declaration in mcp_servers",
    "Replace the server with two custom tools that call Slack's search and channel APIs"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Disable everything by default with default_config, then enable the specific tools in configs: Claude can search and list but not post or delete, and the other definitions stay out of context. A prompt rule (B) is advisory and keeps every definition in context. Without an mcp_toolset entry (C) Claude gets no tools from the server. Custom tools (D) work but bring back the integration code MCP leaves to the provider." },
{ id:"d2-15", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An agent must read tasks from Asana, query the company's internal claims database, and write reports in the team's standard template. The team wants to maintain as little integration code as possible. How should each need be met?",
  options:["Asana through a custom tool, the database through an MCP server, the template as a skill",
    "Asana through its MCP server, the database through a custom tool, the template as a skill",
    "Asana through its MCP server, the database through an MCP server, the template as a tool",
    "Asana through a custom tool, the database through a custom tool, the template in a prompt"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Tools are for your own systems (you own and maintain the code), skills for your processes such as a report template, and MCP for third-party services whose provider maintains the server. A custom Asana tool (A, D) means maintaining a wrapper Asana already provides. The template is a procedure, not an integration, so a tool (C) is the wrong feature for it." },
{ id:"d2-16", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A nightly job submits 30,000 invoice extractions as one Message Batch to get batch pricing, then writes result number i to row i of the invoice table, which a cleanup job re-sorts each night before results arrive. Auditors find extractions on the wrong invoices. Some requests also error or expire, and only those must be resubmitted. What should the job do?",
  options:["Set custom_id to the row number and match results by it, resubmitting the errored or expired rows",
    "Set custom_id to the invoice ID and match results by it, resubmitting the batch on any error",
    "Set custom_id to the invoice ID but read results in submission order, resubmitting the errored IDs",
    "Set custom_id to the invoice ID and match results by it, resubmitting the errored or expired IDs"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Results can come back in any order and carry each request's custom_id and result type; a stable key such as the invoice ID fixes the mapping and identifies what to resubmit. Row numbers (A) change when the table is re-sorted. Resubmitting the whole batch (B) redoes the successes. Submission order (C) is not result order." },
{ id:"d2-17", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An extraction service uses JSON outputs to return product attributes. The schema declares attributes as an object meant to hold any attribute name the product has, such as voltage or fabric, with additionalProperties false as required. Every response returns attributes as {}. Each product's attributes must come back. What should change?",
  options:["Set additionalProperties to true on attributes so that any key it holds is accepted",
    "List the expected attribute names in the description of the attributes object",
    "Declare attributes as an array of objects, each holding a name and a value field",
    "Drop JSON outputs for this field and prefill the assistant turn with an opening brace"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Objects with arbitrary keys cannot be expressed: with additionalProperties false and no declared properties, Claude can only return {}. The documented workaround is an array of key/value objects. additionalProperties must be false on objects (A). A description (B) does not add properties to the grammar. Prefilling (D) is incompatible with JSON outputs." },
{ id:"d2-18", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A social-media tool sends a raw JSON schema in output_config.format, with maxLength 280 on the post field. The JSON is always valid, but about one post in twenty is longer than 280 characters, and the publishing step rejects it. Posts must stay under the platform limit. What should the team do?",
  options:["Check the length in their own code and retry or shorten, as the API does not enforce maxLength",
    "Set strict: true on the schema so that the API enforces maxLength along with field types",
    "Lower max_tokens to about 100 so that the model cannot produce a post that runs too long",
    "Use a pattern on the post field limiting it to 280 characters, which the API enforces"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Constraints like maxLength and minimum are not enforced by the API; SDK helpers move them into descriptions and check them in their own validation. With a raw schema, the check is the team's. strict (B) is a tool setting and still does not enforce maxLength. A low max_tokens (C) truncates the JSON itself. The fix lives in validation, not in another schema keyword (D)." },
{ id:"d2-19", domain:2, type:"multi", select:2, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A team replaces its old JSON approach, an assistant prefill of \"{\" plus a regex parser, with JSON outputs through output_config.format. The schema has a status enum with values such as Open and Closed. Which TWO changes belong in the migration?",
  options:["Set additionalProperties to true so optional fields can appear",
    "Put the required fields last so that optional ones appear first",
    "Remove the assistant prefill of \"{\" from each of the requests",
    "Compare status values case-insensitively in downstream code"],
  correct:[2,3],
  explanation:"Message prefilling is incompatible with JSON outputs, and enum or const values may come back with different capitalization, so compare them case-insensitively. additionalProperties must be false on objects (A). Required properties come first in the output whatever the schema order (B)." },
{ id:"d2-20", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A user clicks Generate and waits for a due-diligence report of up to 90,000 tokens. The backend sends a non-streaming request with max_tokens 128,000, and the SDK rejects it before sending, citing the 10-minute limit for non-streaming requests. The user must get the full report in this session. What should the backend do?",
  options:["Raise the SDK timeout to 30 minutes and keep the request non-streaming",
    "Submit the request through the Message Batches API and poll until the result is ready",
    "Stream the request and collect the final message from the stream once it ends",
    "Lower max_tokens to 32,000 so that the request finishes within 10 minutes"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"For long requests, especially over 10 minutes, use streaming (or batches); the SDK can still return a full Message from a stream. A longer timeout (A) keeps an idle connection open that some networks drop. A batch (B) returns within 24 hours, not while the user waits. A 32,000 cap (D) truncates the report." },
{ id:"d2-21", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An operations agent has 30 tools, all marked strict: true. Requests now fail with a 400 saying the schema is too complex for compilation. Calls to its 6 tools that change production must keep schema-valid inputs. What should the team change?",
  options:["Send each request twice so that the first compiles the grammar and the second hits it",
    "Mark only the 6 production tools as strict, and turn strict off on the others",
    "Make each optional parameter nullable with anyOf so that one grammar covers all of them",
    "Move the tool input schemas into output_config.format so that they compile as one schema"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Limits apply across all strict schemas in a request (20 strict tools, 24 optional parameters, 16 union-typed parameters); marking only critical tools strict is the documented fix. The error is a limit, not a cold grammar cache (A). anyOf adds union types, which count against the limit (C). output_config.format shapes Claude's reply, not tool inputs (D)." },
{ id:"d2-22", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A multi-tenant form filler builds its JSON outputs schema per customer, naming properties after each customer's field labels. Every first request of the day per customer is slow, and a support request with a new layout pays the delay again. Healthcare customers are joining. What should the team change?",
  options:["Pre-compile each customer's schema at 6:00 with a warm-up request so that its grammar is cached",
    "Keep per-customer schemas and turn on HIPAA readiness for the organization before they join",
    "Move the field labels into enum values so that the property names stay fixed across tenants",
    "Use one fixed schema structure for all customers, and pass each customer's labels in the prompt"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"A new schema structure adds compilation latency, while a structure already seen reuses the cached grammar (24 hours after last use). Labels also belong out of property names for healthcare: PHI must not appear in property names, enum or const values, or patterns. Warm-up requests (A) still recompile each new layout. HIPAA readiness (B) does not lift the rule on schemas. Enum values (C) are covered by the same rule." },
{ id:"d2-23", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A booking MCP server rejects departure dates in the past by returning a JSON-RPC error with code -32602 and the message \"invalid params\". When it happens, Claude stops or tries other tools instead of fixing the date. The team wants Claude to correct its arguments and retry. How should the server report this case?",
  options:["As a JSON-RPC error with code -32602 and a message saying the date must be in the future",
    "As a tool result with isError true and a message saying the date must be in the future",
    "As a tool result with isError false and an empty content list for the past departure date",
    "As a JSON-RPC error with code -32603, the internal error code, and the same invalid message"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Input validation and business-logic failures are tool execution errors: a result with isError true and actionable text the model can use to self-correct. Protocol errors (A, D) signal a malformed request or server fault, which models are less likely to recover from, and clients may not pass them on. An empty success (C) hides the problem." },
{ id:"d2-24", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An MCP server adds an outputSchema to its get_invoice tool and returns the invoice as structuredContent that conforms to it. Clients that read structured results work, but an older client the support team uses now shows empty results for that tool. Both clients must keep working. What should the server return?",
  options:["The structuredContent only, with the outputSchema removed from the tool",
    "A text content block only, with the outputSchema kept on the tool definition",
    "The structuredContent, plus a resource link that points to the invoice",
    "The structuredContent, plus the same JSON serialized in a text content block"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"For backwards compatibility, a tool that returns structured content should also return the serialized JSON in a text block. Dropping the schema (A) does not help a client that reads content. With an outputSchema, the server must return conforming structured results (B). A resource link (C) points elsewhere instead of carrying the data." },
{ id:"d2-25", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An internal MCP client approves without asking any tool whose annotations set readOnlyHint true. Users can add third-party servers, and one of them marks a delete_records tool as read-only. Low-risk tools from the company's own servers should keep running without prompts. What should the client change?",
  options:["Rely on annotations from any server, but ask for tools whose names contain delete or remove",
    "Rely on annotations from any server, but ask when destructiveHint and readOnlyHint disagree",
    "Rely on annotations only for servers on a trusted list, and ask for tools from other servers",
    "Rely on annotations from any server once a user has approved one of that server's tools before"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Clients must treat tool annotations as untrusted unless they come from trusted servers, so annotation-based auto-approval belongs to a trusted list. Name matching (A) and consistency checks (B) still trust whatever a server declares. One earlier approval (D) says nothing about the honesty of the server's other annotations." },
{ id:"d2-26", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An MCP server exposes extra admin tools as soon as a user's plan is upgraded during a session; today users see them only after restarting their client. The server is used by MCP clients the team does not control, and the admin tools must not be offered before the upgrade. What should the server do?",
  options:["Declare tools.listChanged, and send notifications/tools/list_changed after the upgrade",
    "Declare tools.listChanged, and return the new tools in the next tools/call result it sends",
    "Declare tools.listChanged, and list the admin tools from the start with isError until then",
    "Declare resources.listChanged, and send notifications/resources/list_changed after it"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"A server with the tools listChanged capability sends notifications/tools/list_changed, and clients re-send tools/list during the session. A tools/call result (B) is not where tools are listed. Listing locked tools (C) offers them before the upgrade. The resources notification (D) concerns resources, not tools." },
{ id:"d2-27", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A team adds a release-notes template to its MCP server. Engineers should start it from a slash command or a menu in any MCP client, passing the release version as an argument, and the model must not start it on its own. Which primitive fits?",
  options:["A resource template keyed by version, which the application attaches",
    "A tool with a version parameter, annotated as read-only for the client",
    "A prompt with a version argument, which the user picks in the client",
    "A tool with a version parameter, which clients set to need user approval"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Prompts are user-controlled templates with arguments, surfaced as slash commands or menu items. Resource templates (A) are application-controlled data. Tools (B, D) are model-controlled: annotations describe them, and an approval step still lets the model start the call." },
{ id:"d2-29", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A loop runs three parallel tool calls in threads. When the permit service throws an exception, the loop stops and the two successful results are lost. The agent should keep going and adapt. What should the tool runner do?",
  options:["Catch the exception, and return a tool_result for that call with is_error set and a useful message",
    "Catch the exception, and drop that call's tool_result so that only the two successful results are sent back",
    "Let the exception stop the loop, and rerun the whole turn from the beginning after a short wait",
    "Catch the exception, and return an empty tool_result for that call so the request stays valid"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Each tool_use needs a tool_result, and a failure goes back as one with is_error: true and a message saying what went wrong and what to try next. Dropping it (B) makes the next request invalid. Rerunning the turn (C) repeats the two good calls. An empty result (D) reads as valid data." },
{ id:"d2-30", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A policy assistant built on RAG gives wrong answers on about 12% of a labeled question set. The team is debating a new embedding model, a new prompt, or a bigger generation model. What should it look at first?",
  options:["The average similarity score of the retrieved passages across the whole labeled question set",
    "The failing questions, checking for each whether the passage holding the answer was retrieved",
    "The answers a bigger generation model gives on the failing questions with the same retrieval",
    "The token count of each prompt sent for the failing questions compared with the passing ones"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Stage-by-stage diagnosis starts by splitting failures: answer not retrieved points to retrieval (chunking, embeddings, search), answer retrieved but ignored or misread points to generation (prompt, assembly). Average similarity (A) does not say whether the right passage came back. A bigger model (C) and token counts (D) test fixes before knowing which stage fails." },
{ id:"d2-31", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"In a parts-catalog assistant, questions that mention exact part numbers such as PX-4471-B often retrieve passages about similar parts, while questions in plain language work well. What should the retrieval add?",
  options:["A larger embedding model trained on more text, used in place of the current one",
    "Larger chunks so that each passage holds the part numbers of several parts",
    "A lexical search next to the embedding one, with the merged results reranked",
    "A higher top-k on the embedding search so that more passages come back"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Embeddings match meaning, so exact identifiers can lose to semantically similar text; hybrid search adds a lexical match for terms like part numbers, and reranking orders the merged set. A bigger embedding model (A) is still semantic. Larger chunks (B) dilute matches. More passages (D) add noise without making the exact match rank." },
{ id:"d2-32", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Diagnosis of a RAG system shows that for most failing questions the right passage is retrieved within the top 50 results, but only the top 5 are passed to the model. Sending 50 passages would make each prompt too long and costly. What should the team add?",
  options:["A reranker that reorders the top 50 by relevance to the question before the top 5 are kept",
    "A larger top-k of 20 passed to the model, as a compromise between cost and coverage",
    "A new embedding model so that the right passage ranks higher in the first search",
    "A rule in the prompt telling the model to say when the passages it receives look off topic"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"When recall at 50 is good but precision at 5 is not, a reranker scores candidates against the question and promotes the right one into the slice the model sees. Raising k (B) adds cost and noise. Replacing the embedding model (C) is a bigger change aimed at a stage that already finds the passage. A prompt rule (D) does not bring the passage in." },
{ id:"d2-33", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"When no retrieved passage contains the answer, a benefits assistant still answers fluently, and employees have acted on wrong figures. Each answer must be traceable to company documents, and to nothing else. What should the team change?",
  options:["Answer only from the passages with checked citations, and search the web when they lack it",
    "Answer only from the passages with checked citations, and say so when they lack the answer",
    "Answer only from the passages without any citations, and say so when they lack the answer",
    "Answer from the passages and general knowledge with checked citations, and flag the gaps"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Grounding means answering from the sources, with citations the application can verify, and declaring when the sources do not hold the answer. Web search (A) cites sources outside company documents. Without citations (C) answers are not traceable. General knowledge (D) is the failure itself, flagged or not." },
{ id:"d2-34", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A multi-tenant SaaS shares one organization's rate limits across tenants. A large import by one tenant gives the others 429 errors, and interactive traffic also spikes at times. No tenant may use another's share, and interactive users must stay responsive, including the importing tenant's own. Imports must finish within minutes. What should the platform add?",
  options:["A quota for each tenant at the gateway, with imports sent through the Message Batches API",
    "A shared priority queue at the gateway, with imports queued apart from interactive traffic",
    "A quota for each tenant at the gateway, with imports and interactive traffic in one queue",
    "A quota for each tenant at the gateway, with imports queued apart from interactive traffic"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Per-tenant quotas stop any tenant from taking another's share, and a separate queue keeps an import from starving interactive requests, including its own tenant's. Batches (A) can take hours. A shared priority queue (B) lets one tenant's interactive spike take the others' capacity. One queue for both (C) lets the import starve that tenant's own users." },
{ id:"d2-35", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A service falls back to the same model on another cloud platform when its primary endpoint is overloaded. During an incident, the fallback failed because requests used a beta feature that the fallback platform does not support. What should the team change?",
  options:["Remove the fallback, as a second platform adds risk the primary endpoint does not have on its own",
    "Fall back to a smaller model on the primary endpoint, which supports the same beta features as now",
    "Check feature parity per platform and give the fallback path a request shape it supports, then test it",
    "Retry on the primary endpoint with longer backoff each time before trying the fallback platform at all"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Cross-platform fallbacks must respect feature parity: the fallback path builds requests its platform supports, possibly degrading a feature, and is exercised before it is needed. Removing it (A) loses resilience. A smaller model on the overloaded endpoint (B) still depends on it. Longer backoff (D) delays a fallback that will fail the same way." },
{ id:"d2-36", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"During a provider overload lasting twenty minutes, every request to a writing assistant is retried with backoff, queues fill, and users wait a minute before seeing an error. A cached-template fallback exists for common requests. What should the client add?",
  options:["More retry attempts with longer backoff so that more requests succeed once the overload ends",
    "A circuit breaker that opens after repeated overload errors and returns an error until it is restarted",
    "A circuit breaker that opens after repeated overload errors and serves the fallback while probing",
    "A larger request queue so that no request is dropped while the provider recovers from overload"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"A circuit breaker stops sending doomed requests after a threshold, degrades to the fallback at once, and lets a few probe requests test recovery (half-open). More retries (A) and a bigger queue (D) grow the wait. A breaker that needs a manual restart (B) stays open after recovery and wastes the fallback." },
{ id:"d2-37", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A fallback router sends any non-200 or unusual stop to a second model. Logs show it also falls back on responses that stopped with model_context_window_exceeded, and the second model fails the same way. How should the router treat that stop reason?",
  options:["Trim or summarize the history and retry on the same model, rather than switching models",
    "Fall back to the second model as now, with a higher max_tokens on the retried request",
    "Retry on the same model with backoff, as the stop comes from a transient capacity issue",
    "Return the partial output to the user as final, and log the stop reason for later review"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"A context-window stop is about the request's size, not the model's availability: reduce the context and retry. Another model with a similar window (B) hits the same ceiling, and max_tokens does not shrink the input. Backoff (C) treats it as transient. Returning partial output as final (D) hides an incomplete answer." },
{ id:"d2-38", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A document-analysis service stays well under its requests-per-minute limit but hits its input-tokens-per-minute limit at peak. Each request carries the same 40,000-token policy manual followed by a short document. What change raises throughput the most?",
  options:["Spread requests across more worker threads so that more of them run within each passing minute",
    "Cache the shared policy manual, as cache reads do not count toward the input token limit",
    "Retry 429 responses at once so that the requests fill the limit window as soon as it reopens",
    "Batch the requests into fewer, larger calls that each carry several documents behind the manual"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"The binding constraint is input tokens, dominated by the repeated manual; with prompt caching, cache reads do not count toward the input-tokens-per-minute limit (only uncached and cache-write tokens do), and they cost less too. More threads (A) and instant retries (C) push against the same token limit. Packing documents together (D) still sends the manual's tokens and mixes the analyses." },
{ id:"d2-39", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"An API in front of Claude accepts requests as fast as clients send them. At peak, its worker pool falls behind, memory grows until the service crashes, and every in-flight request is lost. Clients can retry. What should the service do?",
  options:["Grow the queue without limit, and add memory to the service before the next traffic peak",
    "Bound the queue, and drop the oldest waiting request when a new request arrives",
    "Accept each request, and run it at once on a new worker so that no queue forms",
    "Bound the queue, and reject new requests with 429 and retry-after when it is full"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Backpressure means saying no early: a bounded queue plus a clear 429 with retry-after lets clients back off instead of the service crashing. An unbounded queue (A) is the crash. Dropping old requests silently (B) loses work clients think is in progress. Unlimited workers (C) overrun the rate limit and memory." },
{ id:"d2-40", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A remote MCP server for a ticketing system is used by many employees through Claude. Each action must run with the permissions of the employee who asked, and be attributable to them in the ticketing audit log. How should the server authenticate?",
  options:["One service token stored on the server, with the employee's name in each request",
    "OAuth, with one admin signing in so that the server holds a single broad token",
    "OAuth, with each employee signing in so that calls carry their own identity",
    "One service token per team stored on the server, with team names in the audit log"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Per-user identity and attribution call for OAuth with each user's own grant, so the ticketing system enforces that user's permissions. A shared service token (A, D) acts with one identity's permissions whatever the requester can do; a name field is not authorization. An admin's token (B) gives everyone admin reach." },
{ id:"d2-41", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A team must rename a required parameter of its widely used create_ticket MCP tool and change its format. Several agents built by other teams call the tool, and they upgrade on their own schedules. How should the change ship?",
  options:["Add the new parameter as optional and keep the old one working until clients migrate",
    "Rename the parameter in place, and announce the change to the other teams a week in advance",
    "Rename the parameter in place, and accept both formats in the tool's description for a while",
    "Add the new parameter as required, and reject calls that still send the old one from that day"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Tool schemas are an interface: additive change first, old shape kept until callers move, then removal, so no caller breaks on someone else's schedule. Renaming in place (B, C) breaks callers that have not upgraded; a description is not the schema. Making the new field required (D) breaks the same callers." },
{ id:"d2-42", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A team wants to switch its claims-extraction service to a newer model. Its offline eval looks good, but production traffic includes rare document layouts that the eval may not cover. The switch must not surprise customers. What should the migration include?",
  options:["Switch all traffic once the offline eval passes, and watch the error dashboard closely that week",
    "Shadow the new model on the eval set only, as live traffic would add cost to the migration",
    "Shadow the new model on live traffic and compare outputs with the current one, then canary it",
    "Canary the new model at 50% of traffic for a day, then switch fully if no customer complains"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Shadow traffic exercises the new model on real inputs, rare layouts included, without affecting customers; comparing outputs finds differences before a gradual canary. Switching on the eval alone (A) skips the coverage gap. The eval set only (B) is the same gap. A 50% canary judged by complaints (D) exposes half the customers and relies on them to notice." },
{ id:"d2-43", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A team tests on the first-party Claude API, while production runs on Google Vertex AI for the customer's compliance. A feature that passed every test fails in production, because a request parameter it uses is not available there. What should change?",
  options:["Keep testing on the first-party API, and add a feature-flag check that skips the parameter in production",
    "Run the release tests on Vertex AI with the production configuration, before each deployment",
    "Read the Vertex release notes before each deployment while testing on the first-party API",
    "Move production to the first-party API, which supports the parameter the feature depends on"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Platforms differ in feature parity, so tests must run on the platform and configuration that production uses. A flag that skips the parameter (A) ships untested behavior. Release notes (C) are not a test. Moving production (D) breaks the compliance reason for choosing Vertex." },
{ id:"d2-44", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"Twelve internal apps call Claude directly, each with its own API key, logging and retry code. Security wants one place to rotate keys, enforce per-app quotas and log usage, and the platform team wants to switch models per app without code changes. What should it introduce?",
  options:["A shared client library each app imports, which reads the keys and the model from its own config",
    "A secret store for the twelve keys, with each app keeping its own logging and retry code as is",
    "One shared API key for all twelve apps so that rotation and usage logging happen in one place",
    "A central LLM gateway that holds the keys and applies quotas, logging and model routing per app"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"A gateway centralizes credentials, quotas, logging and model routing in one service, so policy and model changes need no app releases. A shared library (A) still needs each app to upgrade and keeps keys in apps. A secret store (B) covers keys only. One shared key (C) loses per-app quotas and attribution." },
{ id:"d2-45", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A prompt change for a support assistant goes to production through a progressive rollout. The team wants a bad version stopped within minutes, without waiting for someone to notice it. What should the rollout include?",
  options:["Staged traffic shares with guard metrics, and automatic rollback when a metric breaches",
    "Staged traffic shares with guard metrics, and a weekly review of each stage's results",
    "A full release at a quiet hour, with the previous version kept ready for a manual revert",
    "Staged traffic shares with no metrics, each stage held a fixed time before the next one"],
  correct:[0],
  explanation:"Progressive rollout catches regressions early when each stage is watched by guard metrics that trigger rollback automatically. A weekly review (B) is days late. A full release (C) exposes everyone and depends on a person reverting. Timed stages without metrics (D) promote a bad version on schedule." },
{ id:"d2-46", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A compliance assistant answers from policy documents, and auditors must be able to check the exact source span behind each sentence. The current prompt asks Claude to quote sources in brackets, and some quotes do not appear in the documents. What should the team use?",
  options:["Stricter prompting that asks for quotes copied exactly, with an example of a correct bracket quote",
    "A second model call that checks each bracket quote and removes those it cannot find",
    "The API's citations feature on the documents, which returns the cited spans with each answer",
    "JSON outputs with a quote field per sentence so that each quote is returned in a set place"],
  correct:[2],
  explanation:"Citations on document blocks return the exact cited text and its location, which auditors can verify, instead of quotes the model writes itself. A stricter prompt (A) still lets the model paraphrase. A checker (B) removes bad quotes but leaves sentences unsupported. JSON outputs (D) give the quote a place, not a guarantee it exists in the source." },
{ id:"d2-47", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"When the model provider is unavailable, a help-desk assistant shows users a generic error page, and agents lose the questions users had typed. Most questions can wait an hour, and a cached FAQ answers some of them. What should the degraded mode do?",
  options:["Show the generic error page as now, and ask users to come back later and retype their questions there",
    "Answer each question from the cached FAQ, picking the closest entry even when it does not match",
    "Queue all questions for when service returns, without trying the cached FAQ in the meantime",
    "Answer from the cached FAQ where it matches, and queue the other questions for when service returns"],
  correct:[3],
  explanation:"Graceful degradation keeps the useful part working: serve what the cache can answer, and keep the rest for later instead of losing it. The error page (A) loses questions. Forcing the closest FAQ (B) gives wrong answers. Queuing everything (C) makes users wait when a correct cached answer exists." },
{ id:"d2-48", domain:2, type:"single", select:1, difficulty:"hard", source:"generated",
  question:"A nightly job classifies two million support messages. Each request carries the same 3,000-token taxonomy and a short message, and results are needed by 7:00. The team wants the lowest cost. Which setup fits?",
  options:["Synchronous requests with the taxonomy behind a cache breakpoint, run across many workers",
    "Message Batches with the taxonomy behind a cache breakpoint, submitted early in the night",
    "Message Batches without caching, as the batch discount already covers the shared taxonomy",
    "Synchronous requests without caching, using the smallest model that passes the eval set"],
  correct:[1],
  explanation:"Batch pricing and prompt caching stack: the discount applies to each request, and caching cuts the cost of the shared prefix inside each one; submitting early leaves room within the processing window. Synchronous calls (A, D) give up the batch discount. Batches without caching (C) pay full input price for the taxonomy two million times." }
]);
